AD  - Guru Gobind Singh Indraprastha University(GGSIPU), Usic&t, New Delhi, India
AD  - ICAR-IARI, Division of Plant Pathology, New Delhi, India
AB  - Timely and accurate plant disease segmentation is essential for precision agriculture and automated crop health monitoring. Conventional convolutional neural networks (CNNs), such as U-Net, are limited by their inability to capture long-range spatial dependencies, resulting in poor boundary delineation. SegFormer and other transformer-based designs overcome this constraint by modeling the global environment, but their lightweight decoders frequently have trouble with fine-grained lesion boundaries. A diffusion-augmented transformer framework, which combines a SegFormer encoder with a diffusion-based decoder for iterative feature denoising and boundary refinement, is suggested as a solution to these problems. The diffusion module produces reliable and consistent segmentation by enhancing local detail while maintaining global context.The detailed experiments demonstrate that addition of diffusion refinement enhances the performance. The comparisons with standard transformer models confirm its effectiveness. In particular, SegFormer (B0)+Diff achieved accuracy, with a Dice score of 99.34% and an IoU score of 98.68%, surpassing conventional architectures. These results establish diffusion-based decoding as an effective and scalable strategy for advancing plant disease segmentation towards real-world agricultural applications. © 2025 IEEE.
