AD  - Southwest Jiaotong University, SWJTU-Leeds Joint School, Chengdu, China
AB  - Instance segmentation is a fundamental task in computer vision that simultaneously provides object categories, bounding boxes, and pixel-level masks. Existing methods can be broadly categorized into two paradigms: two-stage approaches, such as Mask R-CNN, which prioritize accuracy through a coarse-to-fine pipeline, and one-stage approaches, such as YOLACT, SOLOv2, and YOLOv8-seg, which emphasize inference efficiency through end-to-end dense prediction. However, systematic discussions of how these paradigms differ across practical application scenarios remain insufficient. To address this issue, this paper organizes a scenario-oriented comparative study of four representative instance segmentation models based on public benchmark results and reported implementation settings on COCO 2017 and Cityscapes. The comparison is conducted under common evaluation dimensions, including segmentation accuracy, inference efficiency, and computational complexity, rather than as a fully controlled re-implementation benchmark. The summarized results suggest that Mask R-CNN maintains an advantage in small-object segmentation due to its region proposal and feature alignment mechanisms, while SOLOv2 achieves strong overall mask accuracy and high-IoU performance among the compared models. In contrast, YOLOv8-seg shows a prominent inference-speed advantage under the reported settings while retaining competitive segmentation quality, indicating potential for latency-sensitive applications such as autonomous driving and real-time monitoring. These observations show that model selection should not be determined solely by the one-stage or two-stage paradigm, but should instead consider the specific requirements of the target scenario, including segmentation precision, object scale, inference latency, and computational resources. Based on these findings, this paper provides practical, scenario-driven guidelines for selecting instance segmentation models under different accuracy, speed, and resource constraints. © 2026 IEEE.
