AD  - University of Maryland, College Park, 20742, MD, United States
AB  - Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) models reduce active computation through sparse routing; yet their significant memory overhead due to retaining the full parameter footprint of all experts remains a deployment challenge. To address this, we present Xami, an expert-aware adaptive compression framework that exploits routing heterogeneity to apply differentiated structured sparsity across experts. By profiling per-layer activation distributions, Xami preserves frequently activated experts at higher fidelity while more aggressively compressing infrequent ones, all while remaining compatible with native 2:4 Sparse Tensor Core (SpTCs) hardware execution. Across different MoE architectures, Xami consistently improves model-level compression over uniform 2:4 sparsity with minimal accuracy loss and competitive inference throughput. Our results demonstrate that expert heterogeneity can be translated into practical memory savings, achieving up to 2.4× compression over dense compared to just 1.8× achieved by uniform 2:4, without modifying the underlying hardware execution model, providing a highly desirable path toward more efficient MoE inference.  © 2002-2011 IEEE.
