AD  - Malaviya National Institute of Technology (MNIT) Jaipur, Computer Science and Engineering, Rajasthan, Jaipur, India
AD  - Manipal University Jaipur, Data Science and Engineering, Rajasthan, Jaipur, India
AB  - Crop disease detection plays a pivotal role in precision agriculture, influencing both food security and farmer productivity. This survey reviews recent advancements in artificial intelligence (AI) models-particularly Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs), Transformer-based architectures, and hybrid approaches-applied to image-based disease identification. Through a comparative analysis of over 20 studies from 2021 to 2025, we report classification accuracies ranging from 84 % to 99.8 % across real-world datasets including PlantVillage, RARI, and various crop-specific image collections. CNNs such as ResNet50 and MobileNetV2 have shown reliable performance, achieving over 9 8 % accuracy in controlled datasets. Meanwhile, Transformer-based models and attention mechanisms are increasingly favored for their scalability and ability to capture long-range dependencies, especially in complex or noisy field data. Hybrid models combining CNNs with nature-inspired optimization or attention layers further enhance detection accuracy and model robustness. However, practical challenges remain, including dataset imbalance, model generalization to different geographies, explainability, and edge-device deployment. This review not only synthesizes the current state of the art but also identifies critical research gaps and future directions, including the need for lightweight, explainable, and scalable solutions that can operate effectively in real agricultural environments. © 2025 IEEE.
