AD  - Premier University Chittagong, Department of Electrical and Electronic Engineering, Bangladesh
AD  - Premier University Chittagong, Department of Computer Science Engineering, Bangladesh
AB  - Rice leaf diseases pose a significant threat to global food security, with current computer vision methods locked in a trade-off between speed and accuracy. This study addresses this challenge by introducing an optimized Faster R-CNN framework that bridges the gap between laboratory research and field application, offering a practical tool for early disease intervention. The framework combines three key innovations: (1) a dilated ResNet-101-FPN backbone to retain fine-grained features often lost in down-sampling, (2) dynamic anchor tuning tailored for irregular rice lesion morphology, and (3) a synthetic occlusion pipeline to simulate dense canopy conditions. Evaluated on 1,448 annotated images, the system achieved 79.9% mAP at 28 FPS - a fourfold speed gain over baselines - while excelling in detecting sub-5mm lesions (83% accuracy vs. 24.3% for YOLO models) and maintaining 78.2% accuracy under heavy occlusion. TensorRT optimization further enabled efficient edge deployment at 18 FPS. By breaking the traditional speed-accuracy trade-off through architectural co-design, this work advances precision agriculture in three ways: (1) proving that targeted modifications can enhance both detection performance and speed, (2) setting a standardized benchmark for rice disease detection, and (3) providing a field-ready solution to mitigate the 20-40% annual yield losses in rice-dependent regions. The approach demonstrates particular strength in identifying early-stage infections, critical for timely intervention. © 2025 IEEE.
