AB  - Skin lesion segmentation based on UNet and its variants still faces several critical challenges in practical applications, including the difficulty of jointly modeling global semantic context and local boundary information, irreversible loss of high-frequency details during downsampling, and semantic misalignment between encoder and decoder features. To address these limitations, this paper proposes an improved U-shaped segmentation network, termed GALAF-UNet, which is systematically designed from the perspectives of global-local feature collaboration, information preservation, and semantic alignment. Specifically, a Global-and-Local Attention Fusion (GALAF) module is introduced as the core feature modeling unit in both the encoder and decoder stages, enabling joint learning of long-range semantic dependencies and fine-grained boundary structures to enhance discriminative and structurally consistent representations. To mitigate structural and boundary information loss caused by conventional pooling operations, a Wavelet Attention Downsampling (WAD) mod-ule is developed to preserve recoverable high-frequency cues through multi-frequency decomposition during downsampling. Furthermore, a Decoder-guided Semantic Alignment (DSA) module is incorporated into the skip connections to explicitly model and alleviate semantic conflicts between encoder and decoder features via attention-based adaptive alignment. Extensive experiments conducted on three public skin lesion segmentation benchmarks demonstrate that the proposed GALAF-UNet consistently outperforms state-of-the-art methods across multiple evaluation metrics. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Singapore Pte Ltd. 2027.
