AD  - Chengdu University, Department of Computer Science, Chengdu, China
AB  - In the domain of medical image segmentation, numerous models leveraging convolutional neural networks and vision transformers as foundational components have been extensively researched and implemented. Nevertheless, CNNs frequently encounter limitations in processing long-range sequence information, whereas ViTs exhibit reduced sensitivity to local feature details and face challenges related to quadratic computational complexity, which hinder their advancement. Recently, the introduction of state space models, particularly two-dimensional selective scanning, has challenged the prolonged dominance of conventional CNNs and ViTs as core building blocks of visual neural networks. To enhance the precision of image segmentation based on the VM-UNet architecture, this paper introduces the DA-UNet model. We designed two attention mechanisms: one achieves spatial-channel joint feature enhancement by fusing channel attention and coordinate attention. The other designed the DualAttention module to refine features in two paths at the encoder-decoder interface: one path can better fuse useful information in different channels through the feature map weighted by channel attention, and the other path can adjust the information distribution in the feature map through the weighted operation of spatial attention, enhancing the feature expression of key regions while suppressing the feature interference of unimportant regions. This design optimizes and coordinates local details and global context on the VM-UNet architecture, effectively improving the performance of small lesion recognition and fuzzy boundary segmentation. Extensive experiments were carried out on the ISIC17, ISIC18, and Kvasir-SEG datasets. The findings indicate that DA-UNet demonstrates greater competitiveness compared to VM-UNet in the field of medical image segmentation tasks.  © 2025 IEEE.
