AD  - IPB University, Departement of Agronomy and Holticulture, Bogor, Indonesia
AB  - The rapid advancement of machine learning technologies has created significant opportunities to address agricultural challenges, particularly in plant disease detection. Accurate, efficient, and scalable diagnostic methods are essential for effective disease management. This study presents a Systematic Literature Review (SLR) using Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analysis (PRISMA) approach, focusing on three key areas: segmentation, feature extraction, and classification techniques in plant disease detection. The review highlights the increasing adoption of Deep Learning (DL) models, such as Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs), hybrid approaches, and feature fusion methods, which enhance classification performance and robustness. However, challenges such as limited labeled datasets, high computational demands, and inefficiencies in segmentation persist. The findings underscore the importance of addressing these limitations through advancements in segmentation methods, domain-specific, pre-trained models, and lightweight architectures optimized for real-time applications. Additionally, leveraging semi-supervised learning techniques can utilize unlabeled data to reduce dependency on extensive feature types, improving segmentation accuracy, and developing scalable models to meet the needs of resource-limited settings. By addressing these challenges, the application of machine learning in plant disease detection can achieve higher accuracy, efficiency, and accessibility, significantly benefiting modern agriculture and sustainable farming practices. © 2025 IEEE.
