AD  - School of Computer Science and Engineering, Vellore Institute of Technology, Tamilnadu, Vellore, 632014, India
AB  - Detection and classification of diseases in fruit plants using deep learning have emerged as an essential area of research for enhancing agricultural productivity and food security. This review provides comprehensive insights into 77 peer-reviewed articles published from 2020 to 2026 on the seven most widely explored fruit crops: apple, banana, grape, mango, citrus, papaya, and pomegranate. Despite the fact that state-of-the-art deep learning models have 95–99% accuracy in laboratory settings, they experience 18–25% degradation in performance in real-world scenarios due to dataset homogeneity, binding hardware limitations, and very few early disease symptoms available for inclusion into them. In this article a complete assessment framework is presented that goes beyond traditional accuracy-based metrics and includes analysis related to confidence calibration and temporal consistency, as well as out-of-distribution robustness and adversarial stability. The meta-analysis indicates a significant negative correlation (r = −0.73) between model complexity and deployment success, whereas dataset diversity shows a substantial positive correlation (r = 0.81) with field performance, which highlights the challenges of benchmark-centric datasets like PlantVillage. In addition, there are hardware constraints that limit adoption among smallholder farmers and brief evidence of sustained deployment outside of the laboratory for less than 10% of the considered models. Critical issues for multi-disease co-infections, for detecting diseases before symptoms appear, and for the absence of universalized field-validation protocols are also raised. Going forward, research should focus on lightweight deployment-ready architectures, diverse field datasets, multi-label learning, and standardized evaluation processes to increase practical applicability and impact in large-scale agriculture. © The author(s), exclusively licensed to Springer-Verlag GmbH Germany, a part of Springer Nature 2026.
