AD  - College of Computer Science and Information Engineering, Harbin Normal University, Harbin 150025, China
AD  - School of Communication Engineering, Hangzhou Dianzi University, Zhejiang, Hangzhou 310018, China
AD  - Faculty of Arts and Social Sciences, University of Sydney, Sydney 2006, Australia
AB  - Automatic segmentation of skin lesions is a valuable tool that aids clinicians in disease diagnosis. However, spatial-domain methods alone often fail to integrate global context with local structural details effectively, leading to inaccurate segmentation of complex lesion boundaries. To address this, we propose SAFNet, which enhances feature extraction by integrating frequency-domain methods, thereby compensating for the limitations of spatial-domain approaches. The core of SAFNet lies in three innovative modules: First, the Dual-Stage Frequency-Spatial Attention Block (DS-FSAB) aims to capture global context and precisely localize structural details simultaneously. Its frequency domain branch computes self-attention via the Fourier transform for global information exchange, while the spatial domain branch utilizes depthwise separable convolutions to focus on local features. Finally, spatial channel attention and a gating mechanism fuse the strengths of both branches. Second, the Wavelet-Deformable Attention Gate Module bridges the semantic gap between encoder and decoder. This module employs wavelet transforms to separate low-frequency subbands carrying the overall structure from high-frequency subbands preserving edge textures. Combined with the adaptive sampling capability of deformable convolutions, it generates attention maps to focus on critical regions within encoder features. Finally, the self-attention-based cross-layer fusion module ensures semantic consistency across feature levels. By modeling correlations between high-level semantics and low-level features, it achieves precise cross-layer semantic alignment. Extensive experiments conducted on the ISIC2016, ISIC2017, ISIC2018, and PH2 datasets demonstrate that SAFNet achieves superior segmentation performance, with Dice scores of 92.97 %, 87.15 %, 91.05 %, and 92.44 %, outperforming existing state-of-the-art methods. The results validate the effectiveness of the proposed modules. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.
