AB  - In precision agriculture, precise lesion border delineation is essential for targeted disease control. However, current segmentation methods prioritize global overlap metrics above edge precision and cross-domain generalization. We suggest Boundary-Aware MCB-SwinUNet for plant leaf lesion segmentation across various crop kinds, MCB-SwinUNet is a hierarchical vision transformer architecture improved with learnable attention-gated skip fusion and boundary-specific optimization. This strategy incorporates three significant innovations: (1) learnable SkipAttentionFusion modules dynamically weight encoder-decoder features for adaptive boundary-aware feature aggregation enabling per-pixel boundary precision; (2) Swin Transformer V2 encoder with hierarchical window-based selfattention capturing multi-scale lesion features superior to CNNbased approaches; and (3) multi-crop cross-domain training framework showing that cross-crop generalization is enhanced by domain-diverse training without crop-specific fine-tuning. This approach achieves validation IoU: 0.6417 ± 0.0821 and Dice: 0.7786 ± 0.0634 on a difficult multi-crop dataset of 588 images covering apple, corn, potato, and bell pepper leaves with 10+ disease types, with consistent performance across all crop types (Dice range: 0.762-0.785). Superior cross-domain robustness is confirmed by comparative analysis with single-crop specialized models (UNet++, Attention UNet, regular SwinUNet), which shows competitive performance despite 3× larger domain complexity. Individual component contributions are measured in ablation studies: combined loss (+0.6% Dice), SkipAttentionFusion (+2.2% Dice), and Swin encoder (+2.1% Dice). Consistent generalization across many agricultural situations is demonstrated by per-crop analysis. For precision agricultural applications that require reliable cross-domain disease diagnosis without crop-specific retraining, our boundary-aware framework offers a deployable solution. © 2026 IEEE.
