AD  - Department of Mathematics, Chandigarh University, Mohali, India
AD  - Department of AIT-CSE, Chandigarh University, Mohali, India
AB  - The timely detection and description of crop diseases are essential for maintaining crop yield and meeting the food needs. This research proposes a comparative strategical analysis of some of the mod- ern deep learning frameworks YOLOv5, YOLOv7, Faster R-CNN, and Mask- R-CNN in addition to transfer learning based approaches, ResNet and DenseNet for efficient disease diagnostic system in crops. Leveraging a curated Crop Disease Image Dataset encompassing major crops such as wheat, maize, and rice, the study evaluates each model’s performance across key metrics: precision, mAP, performance speed and symptoms identification. While YOLO-based models that were optimized for real-time object detection, was fine-tuned for disease localization with focus on inference time, Mask R-CNN and Faster R-CNN offered detailed seg- mentation and hence offered a rich understanding of disease spread. Similarly, for classification and detection a modified transferred learning model ResNet and DenseNet were introduced while DenseNet models further demonstrated lesser features reusage which helped to localize minor disease manifestations. The results show that for online real-time applications, YOLOv7 performs better than the others because it has both low error and time consumption, but for fine-fragment segmentation in analyzing disease development, it is better to use Mask R-CNN. This comparative content brings significant information about the applicability of these archi- tectures in different agricultural environments, helping the researchers and the practitioners in the selection of the adequate models according to the needs of precision agriculture and crop disease control. © Grenze Scientific Society, 2025.
