AB  - Accurate segmentation of crop disease regions is crucial for disease monitoring, quantification, and precision spraying. However, disease spots in field images often show large-scale variation, blurred boundaries, and irregular shapes, and are also affected by complex backgrounds and illumination changes. This makes it difficult for existing methods to balance long-range context modeling and local boundary detail recovery. To address these challenges, we propose C2BPNet, a crop disease segmentation network based on Context-Content-Boundary Perception, aiming to jointly improve contextual representation, lesion-region perception, and boundary refinement. Specifically, we introduce a grayscale-guided geometric prior attention in the encoder, which combines grayscale structural texture cues with relative position encoding to construct geometric constraints. This guides the network to focus on structurally consistent regions and effectively suppresses background noise. In the decoding stage, we use a DSWA-based segmentation head to adapt feature sampling to irregular lesion shapes and recover finer boundary details. We conduct extensive experiments on the publicly available PlantSeg dataset and our self-built Downy-MildewSeg dataset. C2BPNet achieves competitive performance compared with various mainstream CNN and Transformer segmentation models. To promote real-world applications, we have developed a visual diagnostic system to segment field images and quantify disease-spot regions. Our code is available at https://github.com/JLab724/C2BPNet.git. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.
