TI  - An intelligent data-driven framework for real-time crop health monitoring using knowledge distillation
PY  - 2026
T2  - Internet of Things (The Netherlands)
VL  - 39
C7  - 102041
DO  - 10.1016/j.iot.2026.102041
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-105045927009&doi=10.1016%2fj.iot.2026.102041&partnerID=40&md5=928512241b3b46f3488e95c232f0eb08
AD  - SERCOM Laboratory, University of Carthage, Carthage, 1054, Tunisia
AD  - National Engineering School of Tunis, University of Tunis EL Manar, Tunisia
AB  - Real-time, accurate monitoring of crop health is essential for optimizing resource use, yields and preserving environmental sustainability. However, deploying state-of-the-art AI models on resource-constrained IoT devices in agricultural fields is hindered by limited computational power, energy constraints, and intermittent network connectivity, which restricts scalability and responsiveness. Unlike existing plant disease detection approaches that rely on cloud-centric and resource-intensive models, this work proposes an integrated Artificial Intelligence of Things (AIoT) framework that leverages knowledge distillation (KD) to enable efficient, real-time plant disease classification on autonomous robotic platforms during inspection or harvest. This framework is based on a hybrid teacher-student architecture that pairs high-capacity cloud reference models with compact, edge-optimized models for both detection and classification, suitable for edge deployment. Accordingly, autonomous robots, powered by AI and IoT, help by moving around the fields, gathering data, analyzing, and quickly detecting anomalies without constantly relying on cloud connectivity. We focus on wheat disease detection during the critical harvest season, enabling timely interventions and minimizing crop losses. Experiments on large-scale datasets show that the distilled classification student closely matches its teacher in accuracy (89.8%), while the compact detection model retains equivalent localization quality at significantly lower inference cost. The student model’s size is reduced by over 70%, while preserving real-time inference on affordable edge hardware, confirming its suitability for real-time inference on resource-constrained devices without compromising accuracy. The framework provides real-time crop health insights by visualizing affected areas of the field, supporting fast decision-making. © 2026 Elsevier B.V. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.
